---
// The managed service. This is the one page on the site written in the first
// person: everywhere else Nojoin speaks as a product, but a service provided
// personally by one named human cannot honestly say "it". SITE.md carries the
// scoped exception and its reasoning.
//
// Every figure here is a commitment rather than a claim, so nothing appears
// that could not be met on a bad week: no uptime number, no SLA, no capacity
// promise. The hardware requirement is quoted from DEPLOYMENT.md rather than
// priced, because a supplier's price goes stale exactly the way a competitor's
// does and a stale price is the error people screenshot.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const docs = "https://github.com/Valtora/Nojoin/blob/main/docs";

// An address rather than a booking page, by decision. A self-hosted scheduler
// was the plan until Cal.com moved its production codebase into a private
// repository in 2026: the public repo became calcom/cal.diy, relicensed from
// AGPL-3.0 to MIT, stripped of teams and workflows, maintained separately, and
// documented by its own maintainers as being for personal rather than
// production use. No image is published for it, and its app URL is baked in at
// build time, so self-hosting it would mean building and rebuilding a Next.js
// monorepo to keep one booking type alive. This page's only call to action is
// not the place for that. An address cannot go down.
//
// It is a Proton alias on the nojoin.co.uk custom domain, delivering to the
// taylan@ inbox. An alias rather than the inbox itself because this address sits
// in public markup and will be scraped: an alias can be burned and replaced
// without touching the mailbox behind it.
const contact = "hello@nojoin.co.uk";
---

<Base
  title="Managed — Nojoin"
  ogTitle="Managed Nojoin — set up and maintained on your hardware"
  description="From £24.99 a person a month: a private Nojoin instance set up on hardware you own, kept patched, watched and backed up. Every meeting, every minute, no caps."
>
  <main>
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="eyebrow">Managed</p>
        <h1 class="display">All the control, none of the admin</h1>
        <p class="lead">
          Nojoin is free, and setting it up yourself is four steps. Someone still has to
          own the server, apply the upgrades and notice when it stops. I built Nojoin, and
          from £24.99 a person I'll do that part on hardware you own.
        </p>
        <div class="ctas">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href={`mailto:${contact}`}>Email me</a>
          <a class="btn btn-secondary" href={`${docs}/DEPLOYMENT.md`}>What you'd be running</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="strip" aria-label="What the fee covers">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="strip-grid">
          <div class="strip-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M2 12s3.5-7 10-7 10 7 10 7-3.5 7-10 7-10-7-10-7Z"></path>
              <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"></circle>
            </svg>
            <h2>Watching</h2>
            <p>
              A check outside your network keeps an eye on the instance. When it stops, I
              find out before you tell me.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="strip-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M21 12a9 9 0 1 1-2.64-6.36"></path>
              <path d="M21 3v6h-6"></path>
            </svg>
            <h2>Upgrades</h2>
            <p>
              One maintenance window a month, batching whatever's shipped since. Security
              fixes don't wait for it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="strip-item">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
              <ellipse cx="12" cy="5" rx="9" ry="3"></ellipse>
              <path d="M3 5v14c0 1.66 4.03 3 9 3s9-1.34 9-3V5"></path>
              <path d="M3 12c0 1.66 4.03 3 9 3s9-1.34 9-3"></path>
            </svg>
            <h2>Backups</h2>
            <p>
              Configured, and the restore tested rather than assumed. A backup nobody has
              restored is a hypothesis.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Support</p>
          <h2 class="heading-2">Same Day If It's Down, Next Working Day Otherwise</h2>
          <p>
            One person answers, and it's me, during UK working hours. There's no 24-hour
            line and no ticket queue. I'd rather name the limit than publish a promise that
            breaks the first time I'm ill.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Hardware</p>
          <h2 class="heading-2">Your Machine, Your Bill</h2>
          <p>
            The hardware is yours, in your building or your own cloud account, and you pay
            for it directly. The practical minimum is 8 GB of VRAM for Whisper Turbo and
            Pyannote. CPU-only works as well and runs slower. If you've already got
            something that qualifies, the hardware costs you nothing extra; if you haven't,
            scoping one is where the first call starts.
          </p>
          <a class="text-link" href={`${docs}/DEPLOYMENT.md`}>Recommended hardware →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="feature-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">Feature Requests</p>
          <h2 class="heading-2">Requests Go to the Front, and Ship to Everyone</h2>
          <p>
            Your requests get read first and get an answer rather than silence. Sometimes
            that answer is no, with the reason. Whatever does get built ships in Nojoin for
            everyone under AGPLv3, because the licence doesn't permit a private version and
            I wouldn't sell one anyway. <mark class="hl">Nothing here locks you in: you're
            buying my time, not access.</mark>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-label="Price">
      <div class="wrap">
        <div class="terms">
          <p class="eyebrow">Price</p>
          <h2 class="heading-2">£24.99 a Person, a Month</h2>
          <dl class="terms-list">
            <div>
              <dt>Minimum</dt>
              <dd>Five people. Above that it's per person, and it stays per person.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Included</dt>
              <dd>
                Every meeting, every minute, all of it. No monthly allowance, no ceiling on
                how long a call can run, no history that expires while you're still paying.
                There's no meter in the software to hit.
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Hardware</dt>
              <dd>
                Yours, in your building or your own cloud account, and billed to you
                directly rather than marked up through me.
              </dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="closer">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2 class="heading-2">Start With an Email</h2>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; margin-top: 0.75rem;">
          Tell me how many people, what you're running now, and what's stopping you using
          something hosted. I'll tell you if it's a bad fit. That happens, and it's cheaper
          for both of us than finding out in month three.
        </p>
        <div class="ctas">
          <a class="btn btn-inverse" href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/compare/">See the comparison</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
